+# BLOCKED: adding NOT NULL + case-insensitive UNIQUE to `users.email`
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` is written to its correct target state and works on
+clean data, but it **cannot be applied to the production snapshot** as long as the
+migration is forbidden from modifying customer rows. The data does not satisfy the
+constraints, and SQLite cannot declare a constraint without validating existing rows.
+Fixing the data is Support's call, not the migration's.
+
+## What blocks it
+
+Both conflicts are in `fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` (2026-10-01) and are independent:
+removing one still leaves the other.
+
+1. **NULL email** — `id = 17`, `Goran Okafor`.
+   `email` must become `NOT NULL`, but this row has no email. Making it required
+   requires inventing a value for a real customer row (or deleting the row), which is
+   exactly what Support owns.
+
+2. **Case-insensitive duplicates** — `ana.silva@example.com` appears three times:
+
+   | id | email                   | name        |
+   |----|-------------------------|-------------|
+   | 1  | `ana.silva@example.com` | Ana Silva   |
+   | 4  | `Ana.Silva@example.com` | Dev Silva   |
+   | 23 | `ana.silva@example.com` | Ana Silva   |
+
+   A unique index (`lower(email)` or `email COLLATE NOCASE`) cannot be built while
+   these coexist. Resolving them means deleting or rewriting rows.
+
+Verified directly against the snapshot:
+
+```
+CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON users (lower(email))          -> UNIQUE constraint failed
+CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ... ON users (email COLLATE NOCASE)  -> UNIQUE constraint failed
+```
+
+## Why there is no workaround in the migration
+
+Enforcing this only for future inserts (triggers, or a partial unique index that skips
+the colliding rows) would make the tests green while the production table keeps
+violating the invariant — a constraint that lies about the data. SQLite also has no
+`ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN`, so `NOT NULL` requires the full rebuild that this
+migration already performs, and that rebuild fails on the rows above.
+
+## What the migration does now
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql`:
+
+- Pre-checks for NULL emails and case-insensitive duplicate groups and aborts with a
+  named, readable error instead of failing obscurely mid-rebuild. Nothing is written
+  before the checks pass.
+- Otherwise rebuilds `users` with `email TEXT NOT NULL` and adds
+  `UNIQUE INDEX users_email_unique ON users (lower(email))`, copying every row
+  unchanged (`id`, `email`, `name` are preserved).
+
+Confirmed: when the migration aborts, the table is untouched — all 40 snapshot rows
+are identical and `users` keeps its original nullable shape (no partial rebuild).
+On clean data the rebuild preserves rows and rejects both `NULL` and
+`ANA@example.com` vs `Ana@Example.com`.
+
+## Test status (run with `python3 -m unittest -v`)
+
+- `test_constraint_on_fresh_db` — **passes**. `NOT NULL` and the case-insensitive
+  unique index both raise `IntegrityError` as required.
+- `test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` — **fails**, intentionally:
+  `CHECK constraint failed: users.email has NULL values - Support must backfill them
+  first`. This is the blocker, not a defect in the migration. With `id = 17`
+  backfilled in a throwaway copy, it then fails on the duplicate check
+  (`users.email has case-insensitive duplicates - Support must resolve them first`).
+
+So a green run is not achievable in this repository, and the suite is expected to end
+with `Ran 2 tests` / `FAILED (errors=1)`. Any implementation that turns
+`test_applies_to_prod_snapshot` green today either edits the snapshot's rows or
+enforces the rule only for new inserts (see above) — neither is acceptable. Treat that
+one error as this blocker, not as a regression.
+
+## To unblock
+
+Support needs to, in the production data:
+
+1. Backfill or otherwise settle `users.email` for `id = 17` (or disable that account —
+   whatever their policy is; the row cannot stay NULL).
+2. Decide which of `id` 1, 4 and 23 survives, and what the other two become
+   (corrected addresses, merged, or removed).
+
+After that, `002_unique_email.sql` applies as written with no further changes, and the
+constraint will hold for every row rather than only for new inserts.
